## Documentation of the example data sets shipped with the package.
## The data are generated by data-raw/generate_stratified_data.R.

#' Simulated Population and Samples in the bootsurvimp Package
#'
#' @description
#' This package contains a simulated stratified population and two samples
#' drawn from it, with missing values of the study variable due to item
#' nonresponse. They can be used to try the bootstrap methods of
#' [rescale.boot.imp()] and [pseudopop.boot.imp()].
#'
#' The population represents N = 2,000 individuals stratified by age group, so
#' the stratum column is the age band of each individual: `"18-29"`,
#' `"30-44"`, `"45-59"` and `"60+"`. The study variable can be read as, for
#' example, annual income. It follows the linear model
#' `y = 250 + 15 x1 + 5 x2 + 10 x3 + (age-group effect) + e`, with
#' `e ~ N(0, 50^2)`.
#'
#' @section Datasets:
#' \describe{
#'   \item{`data_pop_st_imp`}{The population of size 2,000, divided into 4
#'     strata (age groups) of sizes 400, 500, 600 and 500. It contains the true
#'     values of the study variable, so it can be used to compare bootstrap
#'     results with the true population parameters.}
#'   \item{`pop_size_st_imp`}{A named vector of the stratum population sizes,
#'     `c("18-29" = 400, "30-44" = 500, "45-59" = 600, "60+" = 500)`, to be
#'     passed as the `population.size` argument.}
#'   \item{`data_samp_stsrs_unif`}{A sample of size 400 obtained through
#'     stratified simple random sampling without replacement from
#'     `data_pop_st_imp` (overall sampling fraction 20%), with subsample sizes
#'     40, 100, 180 and 80 (sampling fractions 0.10, 0.20, 0.30 and 0.16).
#'     Nonresponse is uniform within strata: every individual of an age group
#'     responds with the same probability (0.75, 0.65, 0.70 and 0.60). This is
#'     the setting of the `"independent"`, `"independent.weights"` and
#'     `"Shao.Sitter"` methods of [rescale.boot.imp()].}
#'   \item{`data_samp_stsrs_uneq`}{The same sample as `data_samp_stsrs_unif`,
#'     but with nonresponse generated from unequal, unit-level response
#'     probabilities given by a logistic model in `x1` and `x2`. This is the
#'     setting of the `"unequal.resp.prob"` method of [rescale.boot.imp()]
#'     (Mashreghi and Deng, 2023).}
#' }
#'
#' @section Variables:
#' \describe{
#'   \item{`unit.id`}{Population unit identifier (row of `data_pop_st_imp`).}
#'   \item{`stratum`}{Age group: `"18-29"`, `"30-44"`, `"45-59"` or `"60+"`.}
#'   \item{`x1`}{A positive, right-skewed auxiliary variable (for example, an
#'     administrative income on file), observed for every unit; usable for
#'     ratio and regression imputation.}
#'   \item{`x2`, `x3`}{Further auxiliary variables observed for every unit,
#'     used as additional predictors for regression imputation.}
#'   \item{`study.variable`}{The variable of interest; `NA` for the
#'     nonrespondents in the samples.}
#'   \item{`resp.ind`}{(samples only) Response indicator: 1 = respondent,
#'     0 = nonrespondent.}
#'   \item{`resp.prob`}{(`data_samp_stsrs_uneq` only) Response probability of
#'     each sampled unit.}
#' }
#'
#' @source Simulated; see `data-raw/generate_stratified_data.R` in the
#'   package source.
#'
#' @references
#' Mashreghi, Z. and Deng, H. (2023). A Rescaling Bootstrap Approach for
#' Imputed Survey Data. Journal of Survey Statistics and Methodology, 11(1),
#' 234-259.
#'
#' @name bootsurvimp.data
#' @aliases data_pop_st_imp pop_size_st_imp data_samp_stsrs_unif data_samp_stsrs_uneq
#' @docType data
#' @keywords datasets
NULL
